//! The compile-error fixtures: every file listed here must fail to compile
//! with an error whose message is `expect`. `build.zig` compiles each file
//! as a test root that imports `validator` (analysis only: nothing is
//! emitted or run) and matches `error: ` followed by `expect` against the
//! end of each error line (`expect_errors = .{ .contains = ... }`), so
//! `expect` is the whole message to the end of the line: from `validator: `
//! on for the validator's own errors.
//!
//! To add a fixture, write `compile_errors/<case>.zig`: a struct `Bad` with a
//! field `x` and the declaration under test, and a test that runs
//! `comptime v.assertRules(Bad);` (or calls `v.check`/`v.checkAll` for errors
//! of the entry points). Then add an entry below. Write `expect` by hand from
//! the text the error is meant to have (for a catalog error, `<Type>` is
//! `<file stem>.Bad` and `<field>` is `x`), never by copying the compiler's
//! output: a copied text would only check the implementation against itself.
//! `row` is the id of the error in the catalog (`G-13`), `variant of G-13`
//! for another example of the same error, or `internal` for an error that
//! only a misuse of the module's internals can reach. Fixtures are never
//! imported by the module or by its tests; they must stay `zig fmt`-clean,
//! since only their semantic analysis is meant to fail.
const Fixture = @import("cases.zig").Fixture;

